---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Sideboard Analysis - Replenish Guide"
>
    <PageHeader 
        title="Sideboard Analysis"
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/decree.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Decree of Silence"
        imageCaption="Decree of Silence"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Sculpting Method" titleTag="h2">
    <p>
        I use this process before every tournament to finalize my 75. Start with
        a pool of 85-90 cards that you think could work in the deck, then carve
        away what doesn't fit the expected field.
    </p>
    <ol style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li>
            <strong>Start Broad.</strong> 85–90 cards including variants and bullets.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Assess the Meta.</strong> Goblins heavy? Wraths. Blue heavy? Grids
            and creatures.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Prioritize Slots.</strong> Rank flex cards by standalone value
            and field impact.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cut Strategically.</strong> Remove cards that don’t serve at least
            two matchups.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Finalize Sideboard.</strong> Transformative packages only if they
            address multiple matchups.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Main vs. Side.</strong> Placement based on expected frequency.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        <strong>The Creature Sideboard Package:</strong> Angel and Mage let you transform
        into a midrange deck after sideboarding. Your opponents face a bad choice:
        keep their enchantment hate and risk losing to creature beatdown, or cut it
        and risk losing to the combo. Either way their draws get worse.
    </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboarding Principles" titleTag="h2">
    <h3>Transformation Against Control</h3>
    <p>
        Against blue permission decks, you become a different deck. Frantic,
        Wave, Intuition, excess Swords all come out. Grid, Angels, Abeyance,
        Denial, fourth Mage go in. You're a midrange threat deck. If they
        sideboard to fight enchantments, they face Angels and Mages. If they
        respect the creatures, they face Replenish.
    </p>

    <h3>Keeping Combo Against Aggro</h3>
    <p>
        Against aggro the combo stays in. You swap interaction pieces. Seals
        come out when there aren't relevant targets.
    </p>

    <h3>Mage Exits Against Green</h3>
    <p>
        Against Rock, Ponza, and Terrageddon, Mages come out. Their creatures
        outclass a 2/2 and they don't depend on single spells Mage can name.
        Angels replace them.
    </p>

    <h3>Exalted Angel Is the Default In</h3>
    <p>
        Angel enters against every non-combo matchup: aggro, control, and
        midrange. It stays out against combo decks (Enchantress, the mirror,
        Elves) where diluting your own combo is too costly. If you’re unsure
        what to bring in against a non-combo deck, Angel is almost never wrong.
    </p>

    <h3>Frantic Search Is the Default Cut</h3>
    <p>
        Frantic exits primarily against control matchups where card disadvantage
        is punishing and discard heavy decks. Stays in against aggro and combo
        where velocity matters.
    </p>

    <h3>What to Trim Post-Board (General Rules)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Vs. Blue / Control:</strong> Cut <em>Intuition</em> and <em
                >Frantic Search</em
            > (to avoid getting 2-for-1'd).
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Vs. Non-Creature Decks:</strong> Trim <em
                >Swords to Plowshares</em
            >. Be careful though—many control decks bring in creatures like
            Meddling Mage or Exalted Angel from the sideboard, so leaving 1 or 2
            Swords is often correct.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Vs. Red Blast / Pyroblast (e.g., Sligh):</strong> Trim expensive
            blue spells (like <em>Fact or Fiction</em> or extra copies of <em
                >Parallax Tide</em
            >) to limit the number of juicy four-mana targets vulnerable to a
            one-mana Blast.
        </li>
    </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboard Card Analysis" titleTag="h2">
    <CardAnalysis title="Defense Grid (3)" image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Comes in against every permission-heavy matchup. It sticks on the
            board, it comes down on a different turn than your threats, and it
            works with Decree cycling.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Exalted Angel (3)" image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            I board this in more than any other card. It goes in against aggro,
            control, and midrange. Basically every non-combo matchup. It stays
            out against Enchantress, the mirror, and Elves where you can't
            afford to dilute your combo. Against Sligh, even playing it
            face-down as a morph blocker that eats a Lightning Bolt is fine.
            Against control, it demands totally different answers than your
            enchantments. Against Terrageddon, if you're ahead on board with an
            Angel, they often can't cast Armageddon.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The Transformational Plan:</strong> Opponents often side out all
            their spot removal (like Swords to Plowshares) and load up on enchantment
            and graveyard hate (Aura of Silence, Tormod's Crypt, Naturalize). An early
            face-down Angel can easily steal games against UW, Enchantress, Rock,
            or Ponza Oath while they hold a hand full of dead anti-enchantment cards.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Abeyance (2)" image="instants/abeyance.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Protects your combo turn by shutting off their spells and non-mana
            abilities. Draws a card too. I bring it in against permission-heavy
            blue decks and in Opal-Wave combo matchups where disrupting one key
            turn is the difference.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Arcane Denial (1)" image="instants/arcane_denial.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Better than it looks through card advantage math. By itself, Arcane
            Denial is a natural +1 for your opponent (their spell is -1, you
            give them two cards which is +2, net +1 to them). But context
            determines whether that +1 is acceptable. If your opponent casts
            Armageddon and it resolves, that’s a +3 for them—destroying multiple
            lands. Giving them +1 with Arcane Denial is far better than letting
            them get +3. Another scenario: you cast Replenish, they counter it.
            Your Replenish is going to give you more than +1, so giving your
            opponent +1 to resolve a Replenish is ok. The similar logic applies
            to a face-down morph that gets countered: Arcane Denial your own
            morph to draw three cards (+2 for you) rather than Arcane Denying
            their counterspell to save the morph (which gives them +1 - a worse
            trade). Think of the card this way and it always makes sense.
        </p>
        <p>
            Arcane Denial enters against combo decks that rely on resolving a
            single powerful spell (Replenish mirror, PandeBurst, Devourer) and
            against control when protecting a critical spell.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Hydroblast (2)" image="instants/hydroblast.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Only for Sligh and Goblins. Countering Lackey on turn one is the
            single biggest sideboard play against Goblins.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        Flex Slots (1 Mage / 1 Seal / 1 Swords / 1 Web)
    </h3>
    <p>
        Additional copies of maindeck cards. Fourth Mage for blue matchups.
        Fourth Seal for Enchantress. Fourth Swords for creature matchups. Extra
        Web for utility lands.
    </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Other Sideboard Options" titleTag="h2">

    <CardAnalysis title="Orim's Chant" image="instants/orims_chant.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            I had a hard time setting up Chant without Tide. Previously I could
            use Chant to force through a spell that would end the game. Going
            down on cards was fine because if the game ends, who cares. But
            without Tide, Decree is harder to set up for a full combo against
            control. I found that they could just let me Chant, let me resolve
            something, then dismantle it afterwards and I'd be down on cards.
            Defense Grid ended up being better than Chant or Abeyance against
            Landstill, Psychatog, and Stiflenought. Not by a huge amount, but
            enough.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Wrath of God" image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Always in consideration. Extra removal against creature-heavy decks
            like Goblins and Elves. It's also relevant against opposing Meddling
            Mage if they're naming Swords, Wave, or both. I've had it in the
            sideboard before and could see it returning if creature decks pick
            up in the meta.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Sphere of Law" image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Has been in the sideboard in the past when Sligh was a heavy
            presence. Four mana to prevent two damage from every red source is a
            hard lock against most Sligh builds. Right now the red matchup is
            manageable enough without it, but if Sligh representation spikes,
            Sphere is the card you want.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Alter Reality" image="instants/alter_reality.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            A very spicy, highly specific piece of tech specifically to combat <code>Gloom</code> from Monoblack Moneyball variants. By casting this and changing the word "White" to "Black" on Gloom, their own spell taxes them instead of you. The flashback makes it extremely resilient to discard.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Disenchant" image="instants/disenchant.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> With the rise of <code>Aura of Silence</code> in the metagame, running exclusively <code>Seal of Cleansing</code> in the sideboard has become a liability, because an opposing Aura prevents you from easily casting your Seals. Splitting your removal suite to include 1 or 2 <code>Disenchant</code> guarantees you have instant-speed answers that don't get shut down by Aura's static effect.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Playing Through Hate" titleTag="h2">
    <p style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        A lot of beating hate comes down to knowing what your opponent is likely
        to have and not walking into it. You don't need a specific sideboard
        card for every hate piece. You just need to adjust how you sequence your
        game.
    </p>

    <CardAnalysis collapsible={true} title="Withered Wretch" image="creatures/withered_wretch.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            <strong>(Black Aggro, Rock)</strong> Wretch is a real problem because
            it's repeatable and it's on a body that can attack you. If you know they
            have it, save your Swords for it. Don't use Swords on a random creature
            if you don’t have to and then lose because Wretch ate your graveyard.
            Prioritize removing it before you start committing enchantments to the
            yard.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis collapsible={true} title="Tormod's Crypt" image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            The easy answer to this is an early Seal of Cleansing. But you can't
            always rely on having one, so knowing how to play through it
            matters.
        </p>
        <p>
            Against Crypt, the key is not putting yourself in a position where a
            single crypt beats you. Don't just blindly dump your hand into the
            graveyard and hope they don't have it. Use Intuition on your
            opponent's end step the turn before you Replenish. When activating
            Attunement, don't discard too many combo pieces if you're not ready
            to Replenish yet. If they already have Crypt in play, one line that
            works well: set up Attunement on the battlefield and hold your combo
            pieces in hand. Then with a minimal graveyard - just enough that
            they'd want to Crypt - cast Replenish. They use Crypt in response,
            wiping your graveyard. But Replenish is still on the stack. Still in
            response to your own Replenish, activate Attunement and dump a fresh
            graveyard. Replenish resolves and brings back everything you just
            discarded.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis collapsible={true}
        title="Phyrexian Furnace"
        image="artifacts/phyrexian_furnace.jpg"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Furnace is different because it only exiles one card at a time.
            Don't commit cards to the graveyard slowly — that just lets them
            pick off your best pieces one by one. If you're going to go for it,
            go for it all at once. Force them to make a choice about what one
            thing to remove. They can only get one card, and if the rest of your
            graveyard is good enough, Replenish still wins.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis collapsible={true}
        title="Tranquil Domain / Naturalize"
        image="instants/tranquil_domain.jpg"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            <strong>Enchantment Removal:</strong> Decree of Silence is the easiest
            answer. If you Replenish with Decree, they need to fight through three
            counters before they can touch your board. The Parallax Wave protection
            trick also helps - with Opal-Wave, you can Wave your own enchantments
            to safety and they come back fresh. Another winning line is getting two
            Opalescences on the board, since you can use one to protect the other.
            Post-board against heavy enchantment removal, the creature package gives
            them a completely different problem to solve.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis collapsible={true} title="Meddling Mage" image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Be aware of which decks can have this post-board. If you expect it,
            keep some amount of removal available. Parallax Wave handles it,
            Swords works, and Wrath of God if you have it. One Meddling Mage
            doesn't lock you out if you know it's coming.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
</Layout>
